Using basic trigonometry, it is not difficult to establish the converstions in 6. 
From the right triangle shown in figure 1.101, we have 

		cos(π/2 - φ) = r/p

Hence, 

		r = p cos(π/2 - φ) = p sinφ

Similarly, 

		sin(π/2 - φ) = z/p

so that
		z = p sin(π/2 - φ) = p cosφ

Thus, the formulas in 6 follow when 0 <= φ <= π/2. 
If π/2 < φ <= π, then we may employ figure 1.102.
So 

		r = p cos(φ - π/2) = p sin φ, 

and 

		z = -p sin(φ - π/2) = p sinφ

and

		z = -p sin(φ - π/2) = p sin(π/2 - φ) = p cosφ


Hence, the relations in 6 hold in general. 
The equations in 7 follow by substitution of those in 6 into those of 3 and 4
